  <div class="page-content">
    <section id="overview" class="section container container--wide">
      <p class="section-kicker">Overview</p>
      <h2>General-purpose manipulation, designed for adaptation</h2>
      <div>
        <p class="section-intro">
          Physical AI models combine the semantic and perceptual capabilities of
          pretrained vision-language models with continuous robot control, but
          reliable deployment still requires adaptation. Broad pretraining exposes
          a model to many robots and tasks without fully teaching the sensing,
          kinematics, action space, and control conventions of any one platform. As
          a result, adapting directly from a general pretrained model can require
          downstream data to teach both the target robot and the target task at once.
        </p>
        <p class="section-intro">
          Rho separates adaptation to an <em>embodiment</em> from adaptation to a{" "}
          <em>task</em>. After physical grounding and multi-embodiment pretraining,
          horizontal midtraining teaches one robot across a broad set of tasks and
          motions. The resulting checkpoint becomes a reusable starting point for
          many downstream policies on that platform. Vertical task adaptation then
          specializes the model from comparatively small target-task datasets, and
          an optional internal latent policy can continue adapting after deployment
          from corrective interaction. We release the{" "}
          <a href="https://huggingface.co/microsoft/rho-base">base model</a> and
          embodiment-midtrained checkpoints for{" "}
          <a href="https://huggingface.co/microsoft/rho-fr3-duo">FR3 Duo</a>,{" "}
          <a href="https://huggingface.co/microsoft/rho-ur-ai-trainer">UR AI Trainer</a>,
          and <a href="https://huggingface.co/microsoft/rho-yam-box">YAM Box</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature-grid">
        <article>
          <span class="feature-number">01</span>
          <h3>Physically grounded VLM</h3>
          <p>A compact Phi-family backbone is retrained with robotics-relevant grounding, pointing, spatial reasoning, OCR, counting, and multi-image visual-question-answering data before it learns continuous actions.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span class="feature-number">02</span>
          <h3>Flow-matching action expert</h3>
          <p>A 542M-parameter action expert conditions on vision, language, and robot state, then transforms Gaussian noise into configurable chunks of continuous actions using flow matching.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span class="feature-number">03</span>
          <h3>Embodiment-first adaptation</h3>
          <p>Horizontal midtraining learns a target robot's observations, kinematics, workspace, and control conventions before vertical adaptation specializes the model to a deployment task.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="adaptation" class="section adaptation-section">
      <div class="container container--wide">
        <p class="section-kicker">Training recipe</p>
        <h2>Regularized progressive adaptation</h2>
        <p class="section-intro">
          Rho's regularized progressive adaptation recipe first grounds a Phi-family
          vision-language backbone in physical-world perception, then couples it to
          a flow-matching action expert through heterogeneous multi-embodiment
          pretraining. Embodiment-targeted horizontal midtraining teaches one robot
          across many tasks, and vertical adaptation specializes that checkpoint to
          a target task. Vision-language batches remain in the mixture during
          pretraining and midtraining to preserve useful spatial and linguistic
          capabilities while the model learns continuous control.
        </p>

        <h3 class="pretraining-mixture-title">Pretraining mixture</h3>
        <figure class="figure figure--paper figure--mixture">
          <img
            src={withBase("assets/rho-tomyum-mixture-figure3.png")}
            alt="Rho-Tomyum pretraining mixture, showing each robot, UMI-style, simulation, and vision-language source with its hours, control frequency, and sampling weight"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <figcaption>
            Figure 3. Rho-Tomyum mixture. It consists of 90% robot and UMI-style
            data and 10% vision-language data. All the robot/UMI-style data is
            bimanual, with the exception of a small subset of Open X-Embodiment
            Magic Soup we call OXE Amuse Bouche. The robot data includes
            trajectories from policies controlling a UR AI Trainer simulated in
            NVIDIA Isaac Sim, trained using RL and the OmniReset framework.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <div class="adaptation-flow" role="group" aria-label="Four-stage Rho training pipeline">
          {adaptationStages.map((stage, index) => (
            <article class="adaptation-stage">
              <div class="adaptation-stage__topline">
                <span class="adaptation-stage__number">{stage.number}</span>
                <span class="adaptation-stage__label">{stage.label}</span>
              </div>
              <h3>{stage.title}</h3>
              <p class="adaptation-stage__subtitle">{stage.subtitle}</p>
              <p class="adaptation-stage__description">{stage.description}</p>
              <dl>
                <div><dt>Data</dt><dd>{stage.data}</dd></div>
                <div><dt>Mixture</dt><dd>{stage.mix}</dd></div>
                <div><dt>Output</dt><dd>{stage.output}</dd></div>
              </dl>
              {index < adaptationStages.length - 1 && (
                <span class="adaptation-stage__arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
              )}
            </article>
          ))}
        </div>

        <div class="adaptation-extension">
          <div class="adaptation-extension__icon" aria-hidden="true">↻</div>
          <div>
            <strong>Optional online extension</strong>
            <p>
              After offline specialization, an internal latent policy can learn an
              observation-conditioned initial condition for the frozen
              flow-matching action generator. In the report's physical experiments,
              15 corrected episodes substantially improve performance on difficult
              initial configurations outside the core offline-data distribution.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="adaptation-details">
          <article>
            <span>Phi-Phy</span>
            <h3>Ground physical understanding first</h3>
            <p>
              Phi-Phy is a 4.68B-parameter model with a Phi-family causal language
              decoder, a <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.14786">SigLIP 2 NaFlex</a>{" "}
              vision encoder, and a two-layer cross-modal projector. Its
              physical-grounding mix augments general vision-language training with
              public robotics-relevant data, reprocessed multi-image grounding,
              pointing, OCR, and counting examples, and synthetic
              difference-spotting and odd-one-out tasks. At this stage the model
              strengthens physical-world comprehension but does not yet predict
              actions.
            </p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <span>Pretraining</span>
            <h3>Learn a shared action representation</h3>
            <p>
              Multi-embodiment pretraining turns the grounded backbone and randomly
              initialized action expert into a general continuous-control VLA.
              Robot trajectories span diverse embodiments, environments, camera
              layouts, action spaces, and control frequencies; source weights
              balance dataset scale and embodiment coverage rather than sampling a
              raw concatenation uniformly. Robot and physical-grounding updates are
              sampled in a 9:1 ratio so the backbone continues receiving spatial and
              visual-language supervision as it learns actions.
            </p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <span>Midtraining</span>
            <h3>Learn the robot, not one task</h3>
            <p>
              Embodiment midtraining focuses the pretrained model on one robot's
              observation layout, kinematics, action space, control conventions, and
              characteristic workspace. Training horizontally across many tasks and
              motions produces a platform checkpoint that can be reused for multiple
              downstream policies rather than relearning the robot during every task
              adaptation.
            </p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <span>Fine-tuning</span>
            <h3>Specialize for deployment</h3>
            <p>
              Offline vertical adaptation fine-tunes either pretrained Rho or an
              embodiment-midtrained checkpoint on demonstrations from the target
              task set. The full model remains trainable, but the data mixture
              narrows to target-task robot demonstrations. Controlled RoboTwin
              experiments show that embodiment midtraining can halve the downstream
              data needed to reach a given success level before performance
              saturates.
            </p>
          </article>
        </div>

        <details class="checkpoint-details">
          <summary>Released checkpoints and training step counts</summary>
          <div class="table-wrap">
            <table class="results-table">
              <thead>
                <tr><th>Checkpoint</th><th>Stage</th><th>Global batch</th><th>Steps</th></tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row"><code>rho-base</code> (Hero2 · epoch 1)</th>
                  <td>Pretrain</td>
                  <td>3,072</td>
                  <td>212,000 (1 epoch)</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row"><code>rho-ur-ai-trainer</code></th>
                  <td>Embodiment midtrain</td>
                  <td>1,024</td>
                  <td>130,000 (2 epochs)</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row"><code>rho-yam-box</code></th>
                  <td>Embodiment midtrain</td>
                  <td>1,024</td>
                  <td>90,000 (2 epochs)</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row"><code>rho-fr3-duo</code></th>
                  <td>Embodiment midtrain</td>
                  <td>1,024</td>
                  <td>68,000 (2 epochs)</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row"><code>rho-robotwin-midtrain</code></th>
                  <td>Controlled midtrain</td>
                  <td>1,024</td>
                  <td>2 epochs</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">RoboEval evaluation policy</th>
                  <td>Task fine-tune</td>
                  <td>128</td>
                  <td>40,000</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">LIBERO evaluation policy</th>
                  <td>Task fine-tune</td>
                  <td>128</td>
                  <td>40,000</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="method" class="section section--tint">
      <div class="container container--wide">
        <p class="section-kicker">Architecture</p>
        <h2>Continuous action generation with a compact flow-matching expert</h2>
        <p class="section-intro">
          Rho conditions on one or more camera views, a language instruction, and
          proprioceptive state. Phi-Phy processes the interleaved image and language
          tokens, while robot state is projected directly into the action stream.
          The model supports continuous state and action vectors up to 32 dimensions
          and configurable action chunks, allowing different end-effector or
          joint-space parameterizations across single- and multi-arm robots.
        </p>
        <p class="section-intro">
          The action expert learns a conditional velocity field that transforms
          Gaussian noise into future robot actions. At inference, Rho integrates
          this field from noise to an action chunk using <strong>10 explicit Euler
          steps</strong>. Every expert block cross-attends to the same projected
          intermediate Phi-Phy representation, which is computed once and reused
          across all flow steps. Architecture ablations select a width of 2,048 and
          128-dimensional attention heads, then reduce the expert from 1.65B to{" "}
          <strong>542M parameters</strong> through shared adaLN modulation,
          grouped-query attention, and a 12-block depth while preserving RoboEval
          success (0.673 vs 0.671).
        </p>
        <figure class="figure figure--paper">
          <a href={withBase("assets/action_expert.pdf")} target="_blank">
            <img src={withBase("assets/action-expert.png")} alt="Rho flow-matching action expert architecture" />
          </a>
          <figcaption>
            State and noisy-action tokens pass through 12 transformer blocks with
            grouped-query self-attention, cross-attention to VLM context, and
            flow-timestep conditioning.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table>
            <tbody>
              {modelSpecs.map(([component, specification]) => (
                <tr><th scope="row">{component}</th><td>{specification}</td></tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="embodiments" class="section section--dark">
      <div class="container container--wide">
        <p class="section-kicker">Physical systems</p>
        <h2>Reusable checkpoints for three dual-arm embodiments</h2>
        <p class="section-intro">
          Multi-embodiment pretraining provides a general initialization, but
          deployment still requires learning a platform's observation layout,
          kinematics, action space, control conventions, and workspace. Rho performs
          this adaptation once through horizontal midtraining, producing reusable
          checkpoints for FR3 Duo, UR AI Trainer, and YAM Box. Each checkpoint is
          initialized from pretrained Rho, trained for two epochs across a broad
          multitask dataset from one platform, and continues receiving
          physical-grounding supervision alongside robot updates. Downstream
          evaluations then adapt each embodiment checkpoint to task-specific
          demonstrations and report both full-task success and partial task progress
          on long, multi-stage behaviors.
        </p>
        <div class="embodiment-grid">
          {embodiments.map((embodiment) => (
            <article class="embodiment-card">
              <img src={withBase(embodiment.image)} alt={embodiment.name} loading="lazy" />
              <div>
                <span class="badge" title="Two arms × (x, y, z end-effector position + 6D rotation + gripper) = 20 continuous action dimensions">Dual-arm · 20 action dims</span>
                <h3>{embodiment.name}</h3>
                <a href={embodiment.checkpoint}>Checkpoint ↗</a>
              </div>
            </article>
          ))}
        </div>
        <div class="table-wrap table-wrap--dark">
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr><th>Dataset</th><th>Episodes</th><th>Frames</th><th>FPS</th><th>Original hours</th><th>Sampled hours</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {midtrainingRows.map((row) => (
                <tr>{row.map((value, index) => index === 0 ? <th scope="row">{value}</th> : <td>{value}</td>)}</tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="note note--dark">
          YAM Box was subsampled to roughly 300 hours, distributed evenly across task
          types, to align its mid-training volume with the other physical datasets.
        </p>
