    <section id="efficiency" class="section section--tint">
      <div class="container container--wide">
        <p class="section-kicker">Supplemental systems measurements</p>
        <h2>Training and inference efficiency</h2>
        <p class="section-intro">
          The technical report defines Rho's architecture and training recipe; the
          measurements below supplement that discussion with implementation-level
          memory, throughput, and inference profiles. Rho supports both full-model
          adaptation and expert-only training. Lower values are better for peak
          VRAM and seconds per step.
        </p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="results-table">
            <thead>
              <tr><th>Framework</th><th>Recipe</th><th>Trainable</th><th>GC</th><th>Peak VRAM ↓</th><th>Time/step ↓</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {efficiencyRows.map((row) => (
                <tr class:list={{ highlight: row[0] === "Rho" }}>
                  {row.map((value, index) => index < 2 ? <th scope={index === 0 ? "row" : undefined}>{index === 0 && modelCitations[value] ? <a href={modelCitations[value]}>{value}</a> : value}</th> : <td>{value}</td>)}
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="note">
          These are observed systems profiles rather than model-quality results.
          Precision, gradient checkpointing, trainable scope, and the number of
          flow samples differ across recipes.{" "}
          <a href="#flow-samples">NFS (also written k)</a> is the number of
          flow-matching samples per training example, not inference steps.
          The π0.5 implementation is <a href="https://github.com/Physical-Intelligence/openpi">OpenPI</a>.
        </p>

        <div class="efficiency-study">
          <h3>Inference latency and deployment footprint</h3>
          <p>
            Rho encodes all camera views and the language instruction once per
            policy query, extracts and projects the intermediate Phi-Phy context,
            and reuses it throughout 10 explicit Euler integration steps. The
            action horizon is a configurable recipe choice; the reported deployment
            profile predicts a 50-step chunk and can execute the full chunk or
            replan after a shorter prefix. This cached-context design sustains a
            &gt;10 Hz replanning loop in the measured setup and is compatible with
            real-time chunking.
          </p>
          <div class="table-wrap">
            <table class="results-table">
              <thead>
                <tr><th>Framework</th><th>Flow steps</th><th>Horizon</th><th>Latency (bs=1)</th><th>bs=4</th><th>bs=8</th><th>VRAM (bs=1)</th><th>VRAM (bs=8)</th></tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><th scope="row"><a href={modelCitations["OpenPI π0.5"]}>OpenPI π0.5</a></th><td>10</td><td>18</td><td>44.8 ms (22 Hz)</td><td>112 ms</td><td>201 ms</td><td>7.4 GB</td><td>8.0 GB</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row"><a href={modelCitations["GR00T N1.7"]}>GR00T N1.7</a></th><td>4</td><td>40</td><td>48.2 ms (21 Hz)</td><td>66 ms</td><td>112 ms</td><td>7.1 GB</td><td>8.4 GB</td></tr>
                <tr class="highlight"><th scope="row">Rho</th><td>10</td><td>50</td><td>83.9 ms (12 Hz)</td><td>126 ms</td><td>186 ms</td><td>11.0 GB</td><td>12.5 GB</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row"><a href={modelCitations["MolmoAct2"]}>MolmoAct2</a></th><td>10</td><td>16</td><td>110.4 ms (9 Hz)</td><td>205 ms</td><td>357 ms</td><td>13.0 GB</td><td>15.5 GB</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p class="note">
            Median GPU model-forward time and peak VRAM for one action-chunk prediction,
            measured in our harness on an NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 (Blackwell) in bfloat16, each
            model at its native default flow/denoising steps. These are model-forward times;
            end-to-end robot-loop latency adds framework-specific CPU preprocessing. Latency
            and memory are architecture-driven and are not a model-quality benchmark. At batch
            size 8, Rho (186 ms) is faster than OpenPI π0.5 (201 ms) and roughly 2× faster than
            MolmoAct2 (357 ms); its 11–12.5 GB footprint fits a single 24 GB GPU.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="flow-samples" class="efficiency-study">
          <h3>Multiple flow samples improve training efficiency</h3>
          <p>
            The report's final LIBERO and RoboEval adaptation recipes use eight
            flow samples per training example. <strong>k=8</strong> means drawing
            eight independent Gaussian noise inputs and flow timesteps for the{" "}
            <em>same</em> observation and target action chunk. Each draw produces a
            flow-matching mean-squared error, and the eight losses are averaged to
            form the training loss for that example. The predicted actions
            themselves are not averaged, and no best candidate is selected.
          </p>
          <p>
            In short: <strong>loss per example = (MSE₁ + … + MSEₖ) / k</strong>.
            The observation's VLM features are reused across draws, giving the
            action expert more supervision per encoded observation at the cost of
            additional action-expert compute and memory. <strong>k is a training
            sample count, not the number of inference-time flow-integration
            steps</strong>; inference still uses 10 explicit Euler steps.
          </p>
          <div class="table-wrap">
            <table class="results-table">
              <thead>
                <tr><th>Condition</th><th>10k</th><th>20k</th><th>30k</th><th>40k</th></tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
